    description: "Overview: Water, of course, is a big part of many great things: you, milkshakes, and water parks to name a few. Even though the ubiquitous liquid is transparent, there's more to see inside. While some molecular bonds last a lifetime, we can break these ones apart with a battery and some thumbtacks in a couple of minutes. Let's split some water. - What: Splitting Water the Easy Way - Time: well, hopefully slowly. We will need…\n\nMaterials:\n- 9V battery (others work, just longer set-up)\n- 2 Thumbtacks (metal)\n- 2 Plastic syringes (no needle, anywhere from 5-20mL)\n- Clear plastic cup\n- Salt\n- Food coloring (optional)\n\nBuild Steps:\n1. Ummm...SPLITTING WATER!? - I know what you're thinking: who wants to get rid of water?\n2. Cut Syringes - Time to catch some gas.\n3. Tack Up a Cup - Take two metal thumbtacks and push them through the bottom of the plastic cup.\n4. Make a Solution - Pour in the unsuspecting water, and give it a color tint if you'd like as well.\n5. Take Apart Molecules - Place the syringes in the water, and pull so that there is no gas in them at the beginning.\n\nLearning Focus: chemistry, electrolysis, h20, water, hydrolyis, oxygen\nOriginal Activity: https://www.instructables.com/Splitting-Water-the-Easy-Way/",
