    title: "Giant Sprinkler",
    summary: "I've always wanted a sprinkler I could take out for a spin.",
    description: "Overview: I've always wanted a sprinkler I could take out for a spin. The giant sprinkler is an amazing shin-wetting device using a wee bit of inertia and centripetal force. It works great in shallow pools, lakes, tubs, and densely packed crowds! Make one and see the truly amazing paths of water as they climb up and out of tubes and spray all over. - What: Giant Sprinkler - Why: the concrete isn't going to water itself - Conc…\n\nMaterials:\n- See full materials list on the activity page.\n\nBuild Steps:\n1. Shave a Dowel - Let's bring this to a point!\n2. Drill a Hole - Clamp it in and drill a hole large enough for the skinny dowel to snugly fit through.\n3. The Crossbeam - Chop off a piece of the skinny dowel that's about 12\" long.\n4. Tube Time! - Nab those tubes and cut two approximately lengths with a plastic saw.\n5. Sprinkle All the Things - This concrete isn't going to sprinkle itself, you know.\n\nLearning Focus: sprinklers, forces, centripetal force, circular motion, water, play\nOriginal Activity: https://www.instructables.com/Giant-Sprinkler/",
