    subject: "Science, Music",
    title: "Universal Bluetooth",
    summary: "Why have one bluetooth when you can have blueteeth?",
    description: "Overview: Why have one bluetooth when you can have blueteeth? With a wee bit of hacking, you can open up a bluetooth speaker and turn it into universal bluetooth for any speaker you might come across. There are products out there for this, but this is your very own hack job, and a great introduction to hacking electronics in general. Bluetooth devices seem to be everywhere, and now you can, too. Grab a speaker and let's start…\n\nMaterials:\n- See full materials list on the activity page.\n\nBuild Steps:\n1. Find a Speaker to Hack - This story all begins with a bluetooth speaker.\n2. Remove the Battery - Every bluetooth circuit will be powered by a small but substantial battery.\n3. Remove Bluetooth Circuit - Check out what's inside.\n4. Prep the Wires - You should have two pairs of red and black wires coming from your circuit board.\n5. Your Sound Jack - The 1/8\" sound jack you have is quite a nifty device.\n6. Attach the Battery - If you've broken the battery line (which often happens), simply solder back to the + and - terminals.\n7. Shrink, Plug, and Play! - You are almost to a fully functioning universal bluetooth!\n8. Pouch for Travel - Your bluetooth circuit is working, but it wants a place to be cozy.\n9. Extras! Extras! - You now have the ability to turn anything into a bluetooth speaker, and one that you hacked yourself!\n\nLearning Focus: bluetooth, speaker, universal, remote, sound, communication\nOriginal Activity: https://www.instructables.com/Universal-Bluetooth/",
