    id: "sample-instructables-018",
    studentId: null,
    studentName: "The Oakland Toy Lab",
    schoolName: "Instructables Educator Collection",
    grade: "5-12",
    subject: "Science",
    title: "Hand Pump!",
    summary: "The literal hand pump!",
    description: "Overview: The literal hand pump! With nothing but a tube and science on your side, you can make water climb skyward. This is a quickie and requires almost no set-up, but is a great introduction to so many fundamentals of motion like adhesion, cohesion, friction, air pressure, and gravity. It's also just plain fun. Learn the technique and you'll learn pure joy. - What: A Literal Hand Pump - Cost: ~$5 for a tube - Time: ~2 minu…\n\nMaterials:\n- See full materials list on the activity page.\n\nBuild Steps:\n1. Fill a Bucket - Looking good so far!\n2. Climb, Water, Climb! - Simply place one hand on top of the tube, and one hand on the side.\n3. The Release - After a job well done, it's time to let go.\n4. Try Other Sizes! - Different diameters make for very different results.\n\nLearning Focus: pump, inertia, adhesion, cohesion, pressure, water\nOriginal Activity: https://www.instructables.com/Hand-Pump/",
    challenge: "Build and demonstrate \"Hand Pump!\" while explaining the key ideas behind pump, inertia, adhesion.",
    solution: "Follow the guided activity flow, prototype quickly, test performance, and iterate until the build works reliably.",
