    title: "Making Color Mash-Ups",
    summary: "Light is one heavy subject.",
    description: "Overview: Light is one heavy subject. You're told that the primary colors in painting are red, yellow, and blue, but then you learn that the cones in our eyes only sense red, green, and blue. All light together makes white light, but all color paint together make brown and black. What gives? The science of colored light is incredible, and there's so much to learn. I was fascinated by a particular project called \"Three Little…\n\nMaterials:\n- See full materials list on the activity page.\n\nBuild Steps:\n1. Separate the Colors - Let's start by separating out our document to the CMYK colors.\n2. Colorize for Printing - Each color is now in its own document, but they appear in black and white.\n3. Printing Pigments! - Load up a printer with transparencies.\n4. Combine Your Color Collage - Re-combine the images and hold up to white light see through them all.\n\nLearning Focus: light, optics, wavelengths, frequencies, science, see\nOriginal Activity: https://www.instructables.com/Making-Color-Mash-Ups/",
