    subject: "Science, Electronics",
    title: "Learning About Motion With Light Painting",
    summary: "Got a light?",
    description: "Overview: Got a light? Then you're ready for the wonderful world of light painting. Light painting is an incredible way to use a camera to make beautiful art and illusions. Here is just a sample of some of the fantastic possibilities. But now you can use it for the study of motion, physics, and kinetics as well. Photography has always been a great way to capture a single instance, to freeze a moment in time. In 1878, Eadweard…\n\nMaterials:\n- See full materials list on the activity page.\n\nBuild Steps:\n1. LED Prep - Your first step is to make your LED circuit, which you can do in seconds.\n2. Blinky Prep - For doing some motion and speed tracking, blinky lights are super useful.\n3. Experiment: Flying Things - Trajectories of flying things are just fascinating, and often too quick for us to really analyze.\n4. Experiment: Rolling Things - Tape LEDs to the edges of different cylinders and spheres, and watch the way they move.\n5. Experiment: Spinning Things - Moving from rollers, there is also a ton to look at in rotational motion.\n6. Experiment: Motion Tracking With Toys - This is a great warm-up to think about the way complex things move, be they machine or alive.\n7. Experiment: Motion Tracking With People - The mechanics of we human machines is fascinating.\n8. Experiment: String and Rope - The grand finale (for now) is looking at string and rope dynamics.\n9. Keep Going! - There is of course an infinite number of experiments you can do with lightpainting that deal with kinetics and motion.\n\nLearning Focus: light painting, led, light, blinky, science, kinetics\nOriginal Activity: https://www.instructables.com/Learning-about-Motion-with-Lightpainting/",
