    title: "Bike Wheel Gyroscope",
    summary: "Why is it so much harder to stay up on a stationary bicycle?",
    description: "Overview: Why is it so much harder to stay up on a stationary bicycle? Why don't Segway tours always fall flat on their faces? How do smartypantsphones know when they're face up? The answers are wheely similar, and all lie in the realm of angular momentum and gyroscopes. They keep spinning things in check. Brilliantly, there have been great science demonstrations using a bike wheel like this one at MIT. Also this one from Ver…\n\nMaterials:\n- Bike Wheel (from dump, thrift stores, old bikes, anywhere!)\n- Thick Dowel (I used 2\" diameter and worked great. You'll need about 2 feet of it)\n- Hose Clamp x 2 (from hardware store, big enough to fit around dowels)\n- Firm Plastic (optional, for guards)\n- Zip-Ties (optional, for guards)\n\nBuild Steps:\n1. Make Some Handles - I started out cutting the 2\" diameter dowel into two 9\" lengths.\n2. Drill Handle Holes - First, remove all extra hardware from your bike wheel so only the axle bolt remains.\n3. Saw Some Kerfs - Kerf!\n4. Hose Clamps! - Oh these things are brilliant.\n5. Make Some Spoke Guards - If you want to avoid spinning spokes + fingers, these are great to add.\n6. Attach Your Guards - We're going to use zip-ties to keep this all together.\n7. Take It for a Spin! - Oh there is just so much to do with a spinning bike wheel gyroscope!\n\nLearning Focus: science, stem, education, angular momentum, torque, centripetal force\nOriginal Activity: https://www.instructables.com/Bike-Wheel-Gyroscope/",
