    subject: "Science, Engineering",
    title: "Bubble Window!",
    summary: "Looking for decor that really *pops*?",
    description: "Overview: Looking for decor that really *pops*? Or something that can add that *burst* of color? A Bubble Window allows the string-puller to create a infinite number of wobbling, ever-changing window paintings. All with a just a wee bit of bubble solution and physics. I made this one for my classroom, but you should make them for any purpose and size you wish! - What: Bubble Window! - Concepts: interference, light, refraction…\n\nMaterials:\n- See full materials list on the activity page.\n\nBuild Steps:\n1. Assemble PVC Frame - Measure and cut your PVC frame.\n2. Prep Your Crossbeam - On the top crossbeam, you're going to add two eye hooks (for supporting the bubble bar), and a pulley in the middle to put a string through to raise…\n3. Prep Your Bubble Bar - Cut a length of PVC that's shorter than your tub is wide.\n4. Add Tub Hardware - We're going to need a way to fasten wire to the tub.\n5. Rig Your Wires - \"Ferrules\" are the name of these little wire holders.\n6. Attach Your String - I used nylon string, but you can use whatever you have around.\n7. BUBBLE BUBBLE BUBBLE TIME! - Pour in some of your bubble solution, and it's time to bubble!\n8. Bubble Colors and Physics - The colors on a bubble are amazing.\n\nLearning Focus: classroom, science, stem, art, bubble, blow\nOriginal Activity: https://www.instructables.com/Bubble-Window/",
