    title: "Tooth Phonograph!",
    summary: "Teeth aren't just for chomping anymore!",
    description: "Overview: Teeth aren't just for chomping anymore! Now you can hear Miles Davis with your molars and The Cure with your canines. With only a few basic supplies, you can tap into the physical grooves of records as they vibrate your jaw, playing music and resonating inside your head. Never before have record stores and dentistry been so tightly linked. Toothfully, you just have to try it. It's amazing. - What: Tooth Phonograph!…\n\nMaterials:\n- See full materials list on the activity page.\n\nBuild Steps:\n1. Cut a Cardboard Base - Trace on of your records including the central hole on a piece of cardboard.\n2. Spear With Pencil - Number 2 pencils are your number one spindle.\n3. Throw a Record On - I found that the spindle hole in a record is just small enough that you first have to remove the metal eraser holder with a pair of pliers.\n4. Make Your Bite Stick - Take the skinny dowel or shish kabob stick, and tape your needle to the end of it.\n5. Spin That Music! - You're ready to jam!\n\nLearning Focus: sound, vibration, record, lp, gramophone, diy\nOriginal Activity: https://www.instructables.com/Tooth-Phonograph/",
