    title: "Homemade Harmonograph!",
    summary: "Finally, a robot to draw for you!",
    description: "Overview: Finally, a robot to draw for you! If you only want to make gorgeous geometric scribbles, that is. With one pen and four pendulums, you're bound for pandemonium with this great way to look at complex geometry, momentum, and art! With a push of the base, you can make all sorts of amazing repeating colorful shapes. You can continue to change weights, drawing implements, and paper for infinite possibilities and designs.…\n\nMaterials:\n- See full materials list on the activity page.\n\nBuild Steps:\n1. Cut Some Wood! - Here's the original design for the basic frame, which is like a table.\n2. Make a Useless Table! - It's like making a table without a top.\n3. Make That Base - Cut two inches of wood the same size.\n4. Eye Hooks and Rubber Feet - Start with your useless table, and screw in the eye hooks with your hand.\n5. Suspense! - Take your string or cord, and it's time to attach the base to the useless table.\n6. The Drawing Arm - Time to add in the drawing component.\n7. Pen and Paper Holders - Glue on some clothespins to either side of the end of your drawing arm.\n8. Throw Your Weight Around - This harmonograph is all about momentum.\n9. Doodle Party! - Draw to your heart's content, or let the robot do the work!\n\nLearning Focus: harmonograph, pendulum, drawing, art, science, education\nOriginal Activity: https://www.instructables.com/Homemade-Harmonograph/",
