    title: "Water Bottle Pressure Prank! (pranked by Science)",
    summary: "Have a drink thief on your hands?",
    description: "Overview: Have a drink thief on your hands? They may be able to sneak around you, but there's no way for them to sneak around SCIENCE! With the quick water bottle pressure prank, you can thwart thieves or fascinate them with physics. Often both will happen simultaneously. - What: Water Bottle Pressure Prank - Does it really work: Well, are you really soaked? - Concepts: air pressure, hydrogen bonding, cohesion - Materials: -…\n\nMaterials:\n- See full materials list on the activity page.\n\nBuild Steps:\n1. Poke Tiny Holes - Grab a water bottle (the taller the better), and grab a thumbtack, pushpin, or sewing pin to poke some holes in it.\n2. Test Uncapping and Re-Capping - Test for bottle for prankability and get a preview of the aquatic carnage that lays ahead.\n3. Air Pressure: the True Prankster - At all times, the air is giving you a tight hug with the force of around 14.7 pounds per square inch (at sea level).\n4. Place for Pranking! - After you're all done science-ing, dry off the bottle with a towel and place in the fridge or on a table so your prank recipient is likely to grab it.\n\nLearning Focus: air pressure, hydrogen bonding, van der waal, forces, prank, physics\nOriginal Activity: https://www.instructables.com/Water-Bottle-Pressure-Prank-pranked-by-science/",
