    summary: "If you got some boogie bottled up, add this recycled rabble-rouser to your ruse!",
    description: "Overview: If you got some boogie bottled up, add this recycled rabble-rouser to your ruse! Whether you're in it for the physics of the phun, this is a great project with youth and adults alike to start getting an idea of resonance, vibration, and the way sound works. Put some science in your ears! - What: Bottle Flute! - Cost: ~$0.05 - Concepts: resonance, vibration, sound, pitch - Materials:- Plastic Bottle - 2 Fat Straws -…\n\nMaterials:\n- See full materials list on the activity page.\n\nBuild Steps:\n1. The Flute Stem - First up is to drill a hole in the bottom of your bottle.\n2. Thar You Blow! - Drill a hole in the side of your bottle and put the other straw (in this case the orange one) in.\n3. The Balloon Soundmaker - Cut off the top of a balloon (I know, it's tragic), and stretch it over the top of your bottle.\n4. Adding Notes - Fold your flute stem (the yellow straw in this case) in two and cut a triangular hole in the side.\n5. Time to Boogie! - With a little practice, you're ready to join the band.\n\nLearning Focus: instruments, physics, education, kids, classroom, bottle\nOriginal Activity: https://www.instructables.com/Bottle-Boogie-Flute/",
