    title: "Wind Turbine Game!",
    summary: "Hold on to your hats, because it's getting windy in here!",
    description: "Overview: Hold on to your hats, because it's getting windy in here! A breath of air? Post-meal flatulence? A raging tornado? Harness them all with your new wind turbine, and the game to build the best blade design for maximum watt production. This is a great activity that can be done with a whole class even with a single constructed wind turbine, and a bunch of kiddo-made wind-catching designs. Jump into the 5,000 year old tr…\n\nMaterials:\n- See full materials list on the activity page.\n\nBuild Steps:\n1. The Base - Cut the PVC for your base!\n2. Wiring Your Turbine - Tape the two wires together, and feed it through your drill hole so it comes out the T-joint on top.\n3. The Motor - In this case, your motor is going to act as a generator, which is pretty neat that they can go both ways.\n4. The Voltmeter - On the other end, strip the two wire ends coming out the drill hole, and solder on gator clips (or banana if you prefer).\n5. Test and Prep! - Test your motor/generator by giving it a spin, and you should see your voltmeter spike!\n6. Make Some Turbine Blades! - This is the fun part, and the great part for students.\n7. Spin, Turbine, Spin! - Lock on those turbine blades and let it rip!\n\nLearning Focus: wind, turbine, energy, electricity, generator, motor\nOriginal Activity: https://www.instructables.com/Wind-Turbine-Game/",
