    title: "Squid Dissection",
    summary: "Squidward we go!",
    description: "Overview: Squidward we go! Captivating the hearts of many starring in such worldwide venues as \"Spongebob Squarepants\" and all the oceans you've ever known, squid are truly amazing. There are over 300 species of these wonderful creatures, and being members of the cephalopod crew, they're also my favorite animal. Let's squid! - What: Squid Dissection - When: Now! - Concepts: anatomy, marine biology - Cost: ~$0.20 per squid - M…\n\nMaterials:\n- See full materials list on the activity page.\n\nBuild Steps:\n1. Prep Your Tools! - Slap on those clean gloves, and get your other implements ready.\n2. Check Out Your Squid! - In (1), we're checking out the mantle where you can see the squid's fins as well.\n3. The Beak - Asking the students where the squid's mouth is will often lead to a lot of answers.\n4. Open Your Squid! - Flip the squiddie over so that you are looking at the siphon.\n5. The Main Organs - There are a lot of small organs inside the squid, a great diagram of which can be found here.\n6. The Ink Sac - Right in the middle of it all will be something slightly iridescent, which looks a little bit like an anchovy.\n7. The Pen / Gladius - Remove the majority of the squid and it's organs so that only the mantle is left.\n8. Chromatophores - Now you can really see the chromatophores on the mantle, which consist of pigment-containing and light-reflecting cells.\n9. The Arms and Tentacles - Cut off the arms and tentacles so students can explore them individually.\n\nLearning Focus: squid, dissection, cut, biology, marine, ecosystem\nOriginal Activity: https://www.instructables.com/Squid-Dissection/",
    challenge: "Build and demonstrate \"Squid Dissection\" while explaining the key ideas behind squid, dissection, cut.",
    solution: "Follow the guided activity flow, prototype quickly, test performance, and iterate until the build works reliably.",
    toolsUsed: ["General classroom maker tools"],
