    title: "Circuit Tiles!",
    summary: "For all the wee Edisons and Teslas to be, circuit tiles are the best!",
    description: "Overview: For all the wee Edisons and Teslas to be, circuit tiles are the best! These are a great way to introduce people to circuits in a fun, ridiculous, iterative way. This is great for just one person exploring or an entire class working together. And your collection can keep growing over time! It's like Pokemon, electronics-version. - What: Circuit Tiles! - Time: ~ 1 hour per 10 tiles - Cost: ~ $1 per tile, depending on…\n\nMaterials:\n- See full materials list on the activity page.\n\nBuild Steps:\n1. Make Wood Tiles - An easy way to make these is to just shop up a 2x4 into smaller pieces.\n2. Drill Screws In - So many screws!\n3. Electronics Time! - From here on out, you can do everything with a hot glue gun, soldering iron, and wire cutters.\n4. Power Sources! - For circuit tiles, I've found that 3V is a good target for many simple electronic elements, so a battery pack with 2 AA or 2 AAA should work great.\n5. Lights! - Oh the lights!\n6. Motors! - Things that move are just the best.\n7. Switches - There are so many types of switches out there!\n8. Potentiometers / Dimmers - The cousin of switches, potentiometers and dimmers work great in series with lights, motors, and buzzers.\n9. Add Other Wacky Stuff! - There are so many things you can find in machines, or that you can make yourself!\n\nLearning Focus: circuit, tiles, electronics, current, volts, power source\nOriginal Activity: https://www.instructables.com/Circuit-Tiles/",
